YAAP: 62-cdi-wiz-gno-mal-neu ... with a warpspeed climb
****
This game featured my most masterful "grab the amulet and go"
ascension run yet.  Eidolos had a few after-the-fact nitpicks, so I
was by no means perfect.

Before we get to all that, I'll give you the narrative of how I got
there.

This game started off with an uninspiring set of equipment:

2x ?earth
1x ?light
   +force bolt
   +slow monster
  u!fruit juice
  u!booze
  b!see invisible
   =stealth
   =shock resistance
   /speed monster (0:7)

Nothing too exciting on that list of stuff.  My first moves were spent
zapping myself with the wand, zapping my pet with the wand, quaffing
that potion of see invisible and putting on my rings.

Despite being a gnome, I headed straight for sokoban.  Along the way I
found a few altars (one of which eventually yielded Magicbane), a non-
cursed amulet (later revealed to be poison resistance), and a tinning
kit.  The entrance to sokoban was annoyingly deep (Dlvl.10).  I didn't
wield Magicbane until well into this game.  Most of it's early service
was as a tin opener and engraving tool.  I stuck with my trusty
quarterstaff until Gehennom, and went with poisoned darts for my non-
magical distance attack.

Sokoban was the BoH version.  Nothing too exciting happened in there,
although i did make a few mistakes with boulder movement, making a
luck-wrecking use of force-bolt necessary.  After leaving sokoban, I
headed for the mines.  Below minetown an Ogre King came into posession
of a /lightning.  This was annoying because a ring of stealth and a
ring of regeneration were both dusted.

Then, mines end (wine cellar version) had a soldier with a wand of
sleep.  Discression being the better part of bravery, I decided to
leave mines end alone until I acquired either reflection or sleep
resistance.

With 79 MaxHP and AC:1, I decided to head for the Castle.  I arrived
there on turn 15,099 with 100 MaxHP and AC:3.  Along the way, some of
my armour was damaged.  Playing hide-and-seek with the minotaurs in
the mazes between Medusa and the Castle was rather exciting.

After drawbridging as many of the castle residents as possible, I did
a dash for the wand.  Crossing the throne room twice, with woken up
soldiers, pathetic armour, and no conflict was tense.

The Castle wand was (0:1), and my numeric luck was zero.  I couldn't
risk asking for a marker.  I spent two charges on b?charging and +2
SDSM.  I bagged the wand then broke for the store rooms (hoping for
some magical accessories).  I somehow got tins of all for dragons
(silver, blue, white, and black).  The throne gave me a magic marker,
granting me the luxary of a wand of wishing (1:2) in reserve.

Next, I made a quick visit to the VoTD to b/u/c stuff before heading
back up to do the quest.  A pair of succubi (Sarah and Tessa) gave me
the necessary experience levels.  After offing the Dark One, I invoked
the Eye for Mines End, got the luckstone, invoked the Eye for Sokoban,
twiddled inventory, then invoked the Eye for the VoTD before heading
back to the castle.

While in the Valley, I found an uncursed helm off opposite alignment.
Bye-bye protection.  Because the RNG has a disturbed sense of humour,
the very next thing I did was read a spellbook that turned out to be
identify.  After some final inventory twiddling, I level teleported to
the VS level, and, with the help of magic mapping, worked my way up.

Orcus went down without too much trouble.  Being silly, I decided to
light up the town.  Bealzebub and Asmodeus were paid off for less than
10gp (each).  When I got to Juiblex lair, there was no Juiblex and a
ghost over a pool of water.  staplegun, where ever you are, bless
you.  staplegun's underwater bones pile had the Orb of Weight, a wand
of wishing (0:1), and every single useful ring I hadn't found yet,
sans conflict.

A castle detour to identify stuff and get un(burdened|stressed) came
next.  Around this time I spent two more wishes (magic marker and ring
of conflict).  After mapping the rest of Gehennom, I had to think.  I
took a break to decide whether or not to keep the Orb of Weight.  Half
physical damage is nice, but one's consumable inventory is severely
restricted by a crystal ball in plain sight.

Now, on to the  Warpspeed Ascension Run.

